README-NETPBM -- the 169 image converters, and how to see them work/dd/CMDS/NETPBM holds 169 programs for converting and transforming images.It is the largest single body of software on this disk and, until now, theleast usable: there was no image anywhere in the collection to feed them,and the directory was not on the PATH, so not one of them could be run byname.  Both are fixed.  SYS/login now puts NETPBM on the PATH, and/dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm is an image written for the purpose.THERE IS A MANUAL, AND IT IS ON THIS DISK.  Every one of these programsbut pnmhisteq has its original man page in DOC/netpbm.  Read one with:    nroff -man /dd/DOC/netpbm/pnmscale.1That is the reference -- every option, from the authors.  What follows hereis the part the manual does not do: what these are FOR.THE SHORTEST THING THAT WORKS  pbmtext os9 | pbmtoasciiThat needs no file at all: pbmtext draws the word as an image, pbmtoasciiprints an image as characters.  Nothing here needs a graphics screen -- thewhole cluster can be driven and seen from an ordinary terminal.SEE A REAL PHOTOGRAPH  giftopnm /dd/DEMO/gulls.gif | pnmfile  giftopnm /dd/DEMO/gulls.gif | ppmtopgm | pnmscale -width 78 | pgmtopbm | pbmtoasciiThe second line prints it as characters.  It is a photograph, so thedithering is busy; that is the picture, not a fault.SEE THE DEMO IMAGE  pnmfile /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm  pnminvert /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pgmtopbm -threshold -value 0.5 | pbmtoasciiThe second line prints a filled circle.  sphere.pgm is in plain (ASCII) PGM,so you can also just read it: the magic number P2, then width and height,then the maximum grey value, then one number per pixel.TRANSFORM IT  pnmscale 0.5 /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pnmfile        halve it  pnmflip -cw /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pnmfile         turn it  pnmsmooth /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pnmfile           blur itMANY OF THESE NEED AN ARGUMENT, AND SAY SO BADLY.  Given only a filename,pnmscale treats that filename as its scale factor and then waits on standardinput, which has nothing in it; you get "bad magic number - not a ppm, pgm,or pbm file".  That message means you left out an argument.  It does not meanthe program is broken and it does not mean your file is bad.CONVERT BETWEEN FORMATSPrograms are named for what they do: XtoY.  So giftopnm reads GIF and writesPNM; ppmtogif goes back.  Twenty-seven format pairs on this disk have beenround-tripped and verified -- out and back with the image the same size:  atk cmuwm gem mgr xbm ybm g3 bmp gif ilbm pict pj tga xpm  fits rast sir xwd fs icon macp pi3 pi1 pcx sgi yuv lispmFour of those change the picture's size on purpose, because the format has afixed canvas: MacPaint is always 576 by 720, an Atari ST PI3 always 640 by400, a PI1 always 320 by 200, and an X11 icon pads its width to a wholenumber of bytes.  That is the format, not a fault.FOUR THINGS THAT LOOK LIKE BUGS AND ARE NOT  * pcxtoppm and sgitopnm cannot read a pipe.  They seek backwards in their    input, which a pipe does not allow, and stop with "error seeking past    header".  Write the file first and give them its name:        ppmtopcx < in.ppm > /dd/tmp/x.pcx ; pcxtoppm /dd/tmp/x.pcx  * yuvtoppm must be told the width and height -- they are not in the file:        ppmtoyuv < in.ppm | yuvtoppm 32 16  * lispmtopgm in this build cannot handle more than 16 grey levels; it says    "depth is too large - try reconfiguring with PGM_BIGGRAYS".  Reduce first:        pnmdepth 15 < in.pgm | pgmtolispm | lispmtopgm  * pbmtopk and pktopbm are for TeX fonts.  They take filenames and a    resolution rather than reading a pipe, and pbmtopk also wants a .tfm    metric file.  There is no .tfm on this disk.THE JPEG TOOLS AND NETPBM DISAGREE ABOUT LINE ENDINGS-----------------------------------------------------`cjpeg' and `djpeg' are here and they work.  They will not pipestraight into netpbm, or take netpbm's output, because they end thethree lines of a PNM header with LF and the netpbm ports on this diskuse CR.  Neither side says so; each blames the data:    cjpeg:     Bogus data in PPM file    ppmtopgm:  junk in file where an integer should beTHREE BYTES ARE THE WHOLE OF IT.  A P6 or P5 header is magic,separator, dimensions, separator, maxval, separator, and `pbyte'patches each one in place.  Going out to a JPEG, CR becomes LF:    ppmmake red 8 8 > red.ppm    pbyte red.ppm 2 0a    pbyte red.ppm 6 0a          `8 8' is three characters    pbyte red.ppm a 0a    cjpeg red.ppm > red.jpgand coming back, LF becomes CR:    djpeg -pnm red.jpg > back.ppm    pbyte back.ppm 2 0d    pbyte back.ppm 6 0d    pbyte back.ppm a 0d    pnmfile back.ppm            PPM raw, 8 by 8The offsets move with the number of digits in the dimensions -- countthem.  For the JPEG library's own 227x149 test image they are 2, Aand E.  Measured 2026-08-29; DOC/STATUS has it at more length, andtools/datatests/netpbm.cases holds a full round trip down.IMAGE DATA AND THE OUTSIDE WORLDIf you redirect a raw image out of the emulator to a host file, expect it toarrive damaged: os9exec turns CR into CRLF on the way out, so every pixelwhose value happens to be 13 gains a byte.  Inside OS-9 nothing is harmed --pipes here are clean -- so do the work on this side and convert to a plain(ASCII) format with pnmnoraw before taking an image anywhere else.SEVENTEEN CONVERTERS CANNOT BE DEMONSTRATED HERE -- but every one of themis now ASKED, and what each says about being handed the wrong format is onthe `noreader' screen (2026-08-30; four of the sixteen had never been runat all).  bioradtopgm, brushtopbm,gouldtoppm, hipstopgm, hpcdtoppm, imgtoppm, mtvtoppm, psidtopgm, qrttoppm,rawtopgm, sldtoppm, spctoppm, spottopgm, sputoppm, ximtoppm, xvminitoppm andzeisstopnm each read a format nothing on this disk can write, and no samplefile was collected.  They are kept, and they are untested.COLOUR NAMES, AND WHY THERE ARE TWO COPIES OF THE DATABASE----------------------------------------------------------Anything that takes a colour by NAME -- `ppmmake blue', `pgmtoppm red-blue',`ppmchange' and so on -- reads an X11 rgb database.  Until 2026-08-27 therewas none on this disk at all, so every one of them stopped with    can't open color names database - reconfigure with correct RGBDEFThe database is now here, and it is here TWICE, on purpose:    /dd/SYS/rgb.txt         the sane name    /dd/SYS/rgb.txt.txt     the name the shipped binaries actually openTHE DOUBLE EXTENSION IS AN UPSTREAM BUG, not a mistake of ours.  PPM/libppm4.cbuilds the path with    sprintf( buf1, "%s.txt", RGB_DB );so RGB_DB is meant to be a name WITHOUT the extension.  Whoever built the OSKport defined it as `/dd/sys/rgb.txt' -- extension included -- and the stringsare still in every binary:    /dd/sys/rgb.txt    RGB_DB="%s"so what they open is `/dd/sys/rgb.txt.txt'.  Shipping the file under bothnames fixes all 169 programs today without replacing a single binary.Verified 2026-08-27: `ppmmake blue 3 2' gives 0 0 255 and`ppmmake "light steel blue" 2 1' gives 176 196 222, which are the X11 values.If the netpbm binaries are ever rebuilt from SRC/netpbm, define RGB_DB as`/dd/SYS/rgb' and the second copy can go.The database itself came from SRC/netpbm/VMS/RGB.txt, which was in the archiveall along -- 738 colours, already CR-terminated.